  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nThe persistent upper ridge over the region is beginning to break\ndown with a low pressure system moving across western Canada. Cold\nfront moving into eastern Montana early this morning will bring\ngusty west winds and a cooling airmass. With temperatures\nremaining very warm overnight, will continue the extreme heat\nheadlines through the morning. The dry airmass and increasing\nwinds will keep fire weather conditions at critical levels, so Red\nFlag Warning will also continue. Winds will also be strong enough\nfor Lake Wind Advisory for today. The changing wind conditions, at\nall levels will bring additional smoke into the region from\nWashington and Oregon, for reductions of visibility and air\nquality.\n\nA second cold front will arrive tonight into Monday as a trailing\nlobe of the upper low moves over the region. This will usher in\nanother drop in temperatures for early in the week offering a \nrelief from the heat and fire weather concerns. There may be an \nisolated to scattered shower or thunderstorm Sunday night with \nthis, but otherwise look for mostly dry weather into the first \npart of next week. \n\nLonger term, ensembles begin to show the trough moving downstream\nas a new ridge becomes established over the Pacific Northwest. \nThis will extend height rises over eastern Montana with northwest \nflow aloft and moderating temperature toward seasonable averages \nlate week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nWhile NBM handled the big picture well across the forecast, a\nblend with NBM90 was used for near term winds this weekend in line\nwith the expected windy conditions supporting the Red Flag and\nLake Wind headlines in effect.",
